*Correction to last week=E2=80=99s issue: Many OpenRouter MiniMax queries a=
re
processed by US-based data centers*

Yorwba added an important content on last week=E2=80=99s *ChinAI
<https://substack.com/redirect/ccc7f034-067a-4828-b596-afb57b8e2089?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*,
questioning the article=E2=80=99s depiction of the geographical pathway for
American developers integrating Chinese open-source models like MiniMax
M2.5.

Last week=E2=80=99s TechFlow article claimed:

*An American developer in San Francisco sends an API request. Data travels
from California, via an undersea fiber optic cable in the Pacific, to a
data center somewhere in China. The GPU cluster starts working, electricity
flows from China=E2=80=99s power grid to the chips, inference is completed,=
 and the
results are sent back. The entire process may only take a second or two.*

But Yorwba=E2=80=99s comment pointed out that, when you look at the OpenRou=
ter
profiles for MiniMax 2.5 or Zhipu=E2=80=99s GLM 5 or DeepSeek V3.2, the mos=
t
popular providers are U.S.-based inference providers such as DeepInfra or
Atlas Cloud. One exception might be Siliconflow, which is a Chinese
inference engine company, but it lists its headquarters as Singapore on
OpenRouter (which means, possibly, its data centers are based in Singapore,
not China). Put simply, Yorwba is right to correct me: =E2=80=9C*Most of th=
e tokens
OpenRouter attributes to Chinese models likely never pass through a data
center in China at all*.=E2=80=9D

This means this passage from last week=E2=80=99s issue should also be scrat=
ched:

U.S. companies will likely be hesitant to use platforms like OpenRouter to
leverage Chinese models: =E2=80=9C*An API request from an American develope=
r is
processed through a Chinese data center, physically passing through China.
This isn=E2=80=99t a problem for individual developers and small applicatio=
ns, but
it=E2=80=99s a fatal flaw in scenarios involving sensitive corporate data,
financial information, and government compliance. This is why the Chinese
model has the highest penetration rate in development tools and personal
applications, but almost no presence in core enterprise systems.*=E2=80=9D

Yorwba=E2=80=99s comment led me to a fascinating *Substack post
<https://substack.com/redirect/550193c3-76f3-4f3c-925b-829bdf1c356c?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
titled =E2=80=9CFlooding the AI Frontier=E2=80=9D, by Ben Turtel, a former =
Google software
engineer. On the process by which developers adopt Chinese open-weight
models, he points out:

*Top open-weight models are hosted by a multitude of **US-based** providers
(like DeepInfra
<https://substack.com/redirect/bf5ef6ef-f81c-4230-bf92-6d2e2b7347a5?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
and Cerebras
<https://substack.com/redirect/d2d17e36-cc2f-4aa7-8b03-c4c47d55efe7?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>)
who process queries in **US-based** data centers. Chinese companies have no
visibility into, and generate no revenue from, this usage of their models.*

Why would Chinese companies do this? Ben goes on to parse through the point
made in last week=E2=80=99s issue: the notion that usage of Chinese open-so=
urce
models will enable them to become a the sticky, default infrastructure
option for global developers. Ben disputes this:

*One prominent hypothesis is that if global developers adopt Chinese AI
models, China could set standards that steer AI development worldwide. I=E2=
=80=99m
skeptical. In the past, choosing open alternatives like Android over iOS or
Linux over Windows meant rewriting nearly everything from scratch. But LLMs
are different: they process and generate natural language, making them
perhaps the most natively interoperable technology ever invented. With
tools like OpenRouter
<https://substack.com/redirect/fed36014-07e1-423f-a134-ee99ca4a86fa?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>,
swapping models is often as simple as changing a single parameter in your
code. The same prompt can be processed by different LLMs. Lock-in doesn=E2=
=80=99t
exist here.*

The *whole post
<https://substack.com/redirect/550193c3-76f3-4f3c-925b-829bdf1c356c?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
is worth reading, as he goes on to ponder whether the rise of Chinese
open-weight models =E2=80=9Cmakes it harder for US companies developing*
foundational AI*, just like cheap subsidized manufacturing undercut US
*factories*.=E2=80=9D
*Thank you for reading and engaging.*

