<p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-siz=
e:16px">Why would Chinese companies do this? Ben goes on to parse through t=
he point made in last week=E2=80=99s issue: the notion that usage of Chines=
e open-source models will enable them to become a the sticky, default infra=
structure option for global developers. Ben disputes this:</p><blockquote s=
tyle=3D"border-left:4px solid #4dd0e1;margin:20px 0;padding:0"><p style=3D"=
margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);margin-left:20px;line-height:26px;fon=
t-size:16px"><em><span>One prominent hypothesis is that if global developer=
s adopt Chinese AI models, China could set standards that steer AI developm=
ent worldwide. I=E2=80=99m skeptical. In the past, choosing open alternativ=
es like Android over iOS or Linux over Windows meant rewriting nearly every=
thing from scratch. But LLMs are different: they process and generate natur=
al language, making them perhaps the most natively interoperable technology=
 ever invented. With tools like </span><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redi=
rect/fed36014-07e1-423f-a134-ee99ca4a86fa?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWC=
zChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);te=
xt-decoration:underline" target=3D"_blank">OpenRouter</a><span>, swapping m=
odels is often as simple as changing a single parameter in your code. The s=
ame prompt can be processed by different LLMs. Lock-in doesn=E2=80=99t exis=
t here.</span></em></p></blockquote><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb=
(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><span>The </span><strong><a hre=
